WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.389
So we'll begin by recapping what the Unreal Engine building blocks are.

2
00:00:05.389 --> 00:00:10.550
Unreal offers many established classes that provide core functionality out of the box,

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:15.839
and going through these will help put the rest of this talk into a better perspective.

4
00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:18.699
We'll only briefly touch on these, as I'd highly recommend that you watch our

5
00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:22.859
previous talk, that being an introduction to Unreal Engine 4 for programmers, into

6
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:26.379
which we do a deeper dive into these topics.

7
00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:31.379
As mentioned previously, UObject is the base class for all UE4 types, including all

8
00:00:31.559 --> 00:00:33.619
assets inside the content browser.

9
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:37.500
They have no physical representation in the world, and they have no built-in tick

10
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.259
functionality. As they've got no concept of the world, they have no ability to

11
00:00:41.259 --> 00:00:44.740
update the frame. They must be given an outer.

12
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.579
This is the object that this object resides in.

13
00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:51.420
If one is not given an outer, it's put inside the transient package, which is the

14
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.280
topmost package used for storing temporary objects, which should not be saved.

15
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.079
Objects can support networking, but they do not come with it set about the box.

16
00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.479
If networking is required, perhaps reconsider whether or not UObject is the

17
00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:06.540
right solution for your current technical requirement.

18
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.459
As an example of a UObject, we have the UExplosionData asset, which is used

19
00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:13.620
to define properties regarding an explosion.

20
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.659
Actor extends directly off of UObject, and is usually accompanied by a scene

21
00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:21.200
component as its route, giving it a physical representation in the scene.

22
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.459
For example, consider an exploding barrel.

23
00:01:23.459 --> 00:01:27.000
This would be an actor, consisting of smaller discrete pieces of functionality,

24
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.519
and a physical representation through some flavor of mesh.

25
00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:34.359
Actors do have networking support by default, giving us access to RPCs like we

26
00:01:34.359 --> 00:01:38.129
discussed earlier, as well as standard variable replication.

27
00:01:38.129 --> 00:01:41.170
They're exceptionally well suited to a component-based architecture,

28
00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:43.890
as opposed to a giant monolithic class.

29
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.890
So at this time, consider the following.

30
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.689
If my current technical design requires either networking or ticking,

31
00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:51.370
go with actors and components.

32
00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:55.280
If you don't require either, consider using the UObject.

33
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.159
Actor components allow us to roll functionality into a single class.

34
00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:02.760
They require an actor to exist, because they cannot simply exist in the world.

35
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.319
They're designed to be part of an actor.

36
00:02:05.319 --> 00:02:08.479
And they're excellent when used in conjunction with Blueprint to expose their parameters

37
00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.250
to design with sensible U-property use.

38
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.810
So as an example, we can add an explosion component to our exploding barrel actor,

39
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.409
and keep the actor class completely unpolluted.

40
00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:21.969
If another actor in our game requires some kind of explosion functionality,

41
00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:24.050
we can simply add the component to that class as well,

42
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.189
without having to duplicate anything.

43
00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:28.469
We can also add U-properties to the explosion component,

44
00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:32.389
to allow design to plug in an explosion data asset in as a field,

45
00:02:32.389 --> 00:02:35.409
letting them balance and tweak the functionality separately.

46
00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:38.810
Actor components are not particularly ideal for networked functionality,

47
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.099
as dormancy can only be set on an actor level, as opposed to a component level.

48
00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:46.099
A dormant actor will send all of its components dormant as well,

49
00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:51.110
so we may end up with erroneous or unacceptable behavior as the project grows.

50
00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.030
A scene component extends off of actor component,

51
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.870
with the benefit of providing a physical representation in the scene.

52
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.669
They can be attached to other scene components as well as bones and sockets,

53
00:03:01.669 --> 00:03:04.870
and they can also be used to detect collisions and overlaps.

54
00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.189
It's the scene component that gives a base actor its physical representation in the scene.

55
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:11.550
An actor itself has got no intrinsic transform,

56
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.550
but it does have a root component,

57
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.169
which is a simple scene component giving us our scene representation.

58
00:03:18.169 --> 00:03:21.750
With the recap over, let's take a look at the player.

59
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.310
What classes and concepts do we need to be familiar with

60
00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.360
in order to get a player up and running?

61
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.620
The Pawn offers the entry point for actors that can be possessed by either a player or the AI.

62
00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.259
By default, the Pawn class gives a physical representation in the scene,

63
00:03:37.259 --> 00:03:41.169
but it's otherwise a plain canvas for us to build from.

64
00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:44.289
Pawns have a one-to-one relationship with controllers,

65
00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:46.650
meaning each Pawn can only have one controller,

66
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.300
and each controller can only control one Pawn.

67
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.620
It's important to note that the Pawn is not assumed to be humanoid,

68
00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.699
and it does not provide any default movement,

69
00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:58.259
skeletal mesh, or collision components.

70
00:03:58.259 --> 00:04:00.740
It only provides a default scene component,

71
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.629
that being its root component.

72
00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:05.990
The Pawn extends off of the actor class,

73
00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:08.509
and as such has access to some of its core functionality,

74
00:04:08.509 --> 00:04:12.110
such as its location and rotation manipulation.

75
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.069
In Blueprints, we can quite easily add movement to our Pawns

76
00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:19.060
using the set actor location function like we just discussed.

77
00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.740
This function allows us to either teleport the Pawn from its current location,

78
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.389
or we can perform what we call a sweeping move.

79
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:29.870
The sweeping move essentially slides the Pawn from point A to point B,

80
00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.769
stopping as soon as they collide with any geometry.

81
00:04:33.769 --> 00:04:37.930
Pawns spawned during gameplay are not automatically possessed by a controller.

82
00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.889
However, we do have some options exposed out,

83
00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:41.889
so we can have some semblance of control.

84
00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:45.420
So let's take a look at those in the editor.

85
00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.899
So whilst Pawn gives us a base for all controllable actors,

86
00:04:48.899 --> 00:04:52.339
the default Pawn and spectator Pawns build on top of that

87
00:04:52.339 --> 00:04:55.360
to provide us some out-of-the-box functionality.

88
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.279
The default Pawn provides us with the following components.

89
00:04:58.279 --> 00:05:01.160
We're gonna get a default Pawn movement component,

90
00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.639
a spherical collision component, and a mesh component.

91
00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:07.600
It also provides built-in functionality

92
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.759
to add default movement bindings, which are on by default,

93
00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:11.680
cutting out the need

94
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.199
for any manual adding of movement logic to your Pawn,

95
00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:17.199
whether that be native or Blueprint.

96
00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:18.839
The movement that it offers is as follows.

97
00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:23.120
We get forwards, backwards, left, right, and jump.

98
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.720
The default Pawn movement component

99
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.720
offers a no-gravity, flying-style movement

100
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.639
as the Pawn is not assumed to be humanoid.

101
00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:32.360
The default Pawn movement component

102
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.920
exposes controls over maximum speed,

103
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.389
acceleration, and deceleration to Blueprint.

104
00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:41.189
The spectator Pawn, on the other hand,

105
00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:43.670
is a subclass of default Pawn

106
00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.269
and provides us with a simple framework

107
00:05:45.269 --> 00:05:48.740
ideal for spectating functionality.

108
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.579
Like default Pawn, it offers much the same components,

109
00:05:51.579 --> 00:05:53.420
but it does away with a mesh component,

110
00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.949
as spectators typically don't have a mesh.

111
00:05:56.949 --> 00:05:58.589
The spectator Pawn does, however,

112
00:05:58.589 --> 00:06:00.550
provide its own movement component,

113
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.509
that being the spectator Pawn movement component.

114
00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:04.670
And it's largely the same

115
00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.470
as the default Pawn's movement component,

116
00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:07.949
with added functionality of handling

117
00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:11.610
or ignoring time dilation as necessary.

118
00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.170
A character is a humanoid-style Pawn,

119
00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:18.339
and it is assumed to be vertically orientated.

120
00:06:18.339 --> 00:06:20.740
The character brings with it its own movement component,

121
00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.579
which offers a greater range of movement

122
00:06:22.579 --> 00:06:24.379
than the Pawn movement component,

123
00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:27.620
allowing characters to walk, run, jump, fly, fall,

124
00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.259
and swim through the world.

125
00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:32.980
The character movement component is heavily networked,

126
00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:34.339
making it the perfect type to use

127
00:06:34.339 --> 00:06:37.620
when thinking about un-networked games.

128
00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.819
The character is the first class along the Pawn hierarchy

129
00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:44.139
to provide specific functions for crouching and jumping,

130
00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:47.060
as well as overriding several variables and functions

131
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.500
to specifically facilitate replication

132
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.500
inside the character movement component.

133
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.579
Unlike Pawns, the character comes

134
00:06:54.579 --> 00:06:55.779
with a skeletal mesh component,

135
00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.620
which enables us to make use of animations.

136
00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.699
The native skeletal mesh provided by the character

137
00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:02.180
is also the main skeletal mesh

138
00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.019
associated with the character,

139
00:07:04.019 --> 00:07:05.660
but more skeletal meshes can be added

140
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.899
to the character as we see fit.

141
00:07:08.899 --> 00:07:11.420
The character also offers some animation functionality,

142
00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:14.420
such as allowing us to play and stop animation montages,

143
00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.120
both in native and in blueprint.

144
00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.439
It also provides a capsule collision component

145
00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:22.040
straight out of the box.

146
00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.120
As mentioned previously,

147
00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.279
the character is assumed to be vertically orientated,

148
00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:28.040
and this is due to the functionality provided

149
00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.040
by the capsule collision component,

150
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.399
which in order to calculate complicated geometries

151
00:07:32.399 --> 00:07:34.680
make this assumption.

152
00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.079
The character movement component is a component

153
00:07:37.079 --> 00:07:40.000
specially created to be paired with a character.

154
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.079
It provides encapsulated movement systems

155
00:07:42.079 --> 00:07:43.240
for humanoid characters,

156
00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.160
which are assumed to be vertically orientated.

157
00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.399
It handles movements such as walking, running, swimming,

158
00:07:49.399 --> 00:07:50.920
falling, or flying,

159
00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.839
and it's built with networking in mind

160
00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:54.279
with the aforementioned movement modes

161
00:07:54.279 --> 00:07:57.879
built to replicate straight out of the box.

162
00:07:57.879 --> 00:07:58.759
As mentioned before,

163
00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:01.240
it is created to be paired with the character in mind,

164
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.720
as the character class overrides variables and functions

165
00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.279
that facilitate the networking side

166
00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:07.860
of the movement component.

167
00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.740
Custom movement modes can be created

168
00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:10.579
and can be handled

169
00:08:10.579 --> 00:08:12.779
by the character movement component directly,

170
00:08:12.779 --> 00:08:14.420
giving the developer complete control

171
00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.379
over how their custom movement mode

172
00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:17.339
is handled when it comes

173
00:08:17.339 --> 00:08:19.220
to updating the character's position,

174
00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.060
as each movement mode handles its own movement logic.

175
00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.740
For example, if the movement mode is move walking,

176
00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.220
then it will call the fizz walking function.

177
00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.100
The character movement component isn't blueprintable,

178
00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.740
so for handling custom movement modes

179
00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.860
outside of native implementations,

180
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:36.820
it's usually recommended

181
00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.899
to handle it inside the character blueprint directly

182
00:08:38.899 --> 00:08:41.700
using the update custom movement blueprint event

183
00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.899
that's fired when a custom movement mode is processed.

184
00:08:45.899 --> 00:08:48.419
So now that we know what a pawn and character is,

185
00:08:48.419 --> 00:08:51.700
we naturally have to discuss controllers.

186
00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.860
Controllers are a non-physical actor

187
00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.490
that possess pawns or a pawn derived class.

188
00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:00.169
This is done through the possess and unpossess functions,

189
00:09:00.169 --> 00:09:04.659
both of which are available in native and blueprint.

190
00:09:04.659 --> 00:09:08.100
The base class for controllers is the AController class,

191
00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.340
and this class defines some of the core functionality

192
00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:11.580
of a controller,

193
00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.019
and also make use of the class specifies abstract

194
00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:16.610
and non-placeable.

195
00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:18.850
The form of these specifies works as you'd expect,

196
00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.529
it marks the class as abstract

197
00:09:20.529 --> 00:09:23.370
and therefore cannot be instantiated directly,

198
00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.610
while the latter marks the class

199
00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:29.629
so that it cannot be clicked and dragged into the editor.

200
00:09:29.629 --> 00:09:30.629
As discussed previously,

201
00:09:30.629 --> 00:09:33.190
they have a one-to-one relationship with a pawn,

202
00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:35.750
and this is usually desired by most game types,

203
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.389
but they may not meet all requirements

204
00:09:37.389 --> 00:09:42.059
where possession of multiple pawns may be desired.

205
00:09:42.059 --> 00:09:44.620
Controllers also receive many notifications of events

206
00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.059
that occur for the pawn,

207
00:09:46.059 --> 00:09:47.419
and this gives developers the opportunity

208
00:09:47.419 --> 00:09:49.580
to implement functionality for the given event

209
00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:53.019
and supersede the behaviour that the pawn may trigger.

210
00:09:53.019 --> 00:09:55.220
It's also possible to make the controller tick

211
00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.580
before the controlled pawn,

212
00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.100
which can be used to minimise latency

213
00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.820
between input processing and the pawn's movement,

214
00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.580
an important thing when it comes to player controllers.

215
00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.460
Controllers do not expose transform functions

216
00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.659
to derived classes in native.

217
00:10:08.659 --> 00:10:10.220
Controllers attach themselves to their pawn

218
00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:14.029
when they possess and detach when they unpossess.

219
00:10:14.029 --> 00:10:15.149
So what types of controller

220
00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:17.309
does Unreal give us out of the box?

221
00:10:17.309 --> 00:10:18.710
Well, as the title suggests,

222
00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:23.129
Unreal gives us the player controller and the AI controller.

223
00:10:23.129 --> 00:10:26.799
Player controllers represent the human player's will.

224
00:10:26.799 --> 00:10:28.159
Player controllers are flexible

225
00:10:28.159 --> 00:10:29.600
and a key tool for developers

226
00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.620
when considering the gameplay loops of their games.

227
00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.580
For instance, they can be used

228
00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:37.740
to handle the input notifications rather than the pawn.

229
00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.659
It's important to consider what functionality

230
00:10:39.659 --> 00:10:42.659
should be placed in the player controller over the pawn.

231
00:10:42.659 --> 00:10:44.100
So for example, if you have a game

232
00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.620
in which the player can swap around many characters,

233
00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.019
then it would make sense to implement

234
00:10:48.019 --> 00:10:50.019
the input handling within the controller,

235
00:10:50.019 --> 00:10:52.179
as the controller can simply relay those commands

236
00:10:52.179 --> 00:10:55.019
onto the currently controlled pawn.

237
00:10:55.019 --> 00:10:57.100
The player controller, unlike the pawn,

238
00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.899
persists as only one is created for that player

239
00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:01.700
when they're loaded into the map,

240
00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.559
whereas pawns are transient.

241
00:11:04.559 --> 00:11:06.000
If your game has a gameplay loop

242
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.759
in which the player's character dies and respawns,

243
00:11:08.759 --> 00:11:11.399
Unreal will destroy the current pawn upon death

244
00:11:11.399 --> 00:11:13.440
and a new one will be assigned to the player

245
00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.399
when respawning, but importantly,

246
00:11:15.399 --> 00:11:16.279
it will be controlled

247
00:11:16.279 --> 00:11:19.500
by the player's existing player controller.

248
00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.899
AI controllers, on the other hand,

249
00:11:20.899 --> 00:11:22.419
are exactly as you'd expect.

250
00:11:22.419 --> 00:11:25.539
They're the simulated will of an AI agent.
